          "answer_excerpt": "ALPHA code is MAPLE-726242.\nBETA code is RIVER-553970.\nGAMMA code is CLOUD-400970.\n\n# High-Performance LLM Inference: A Technical Deep Dive into Continuous Batching, KV Caches, and Advanced Decoding Strategies\n\nThe deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in production environments presents a unique set of engineering challenges that differ significantly from traditional machine learning inference tasks. Unlike classification or regression models, which typically process fixed-size inputs and produce fixed-size outputs in a single forward pass, LLMs are autoregressive. They generate text token-by-token, where each new token depends on the entire sequence of previously generated tokens. This sequential dependency creates a fundamental tension between latency (the time it takes to generate the first token and subsequent tokens) and throughput (the number of tokens or requests processed per second).\n\nTo resolve this tension, modern LLM inference servers have evolved a sophisticated stack of optimizations. This tutorial explores the core mechanisms that define high-performance inference: the Key-Value (KV) Cache, Continuous Batching, Prefix Reuse, and Speculative Decoding. We will examine how these components interact, the trade-offs they introduce, and how they collectively enable scalable, low-latency AI services.\n\n## 1. The Foundation: The Key-Value (KV) Cache\n\nTo understand modern inference optimization, one must first understand the computational bottleneck of the Transformer architecture. In a standard Transformer decoder, the self-attention mechanism allows each token to attend to all previous tokens in the sequence. Mathematically, for a query vector $Q$ and key vector $K$, the attention score is calculated as $\\text{softmax}(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}})V$.\n\nIf we were to recompute the attention for the entire sequence at every generation step, the computational complexity would be $O(N^2)$ for each new token, where $N$ is the sequence length. This is prohibitively expensive. The **KV Cache** solves this by storing the Key ($K$) and Value ($V$) vectors computed for previous tokens.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen the model processes the initial prompt (the \"prefill\" phase), it computes the $K$ and $V$ vectors for every token in the prompt and stores them in GPU memory. When generating the first new token, the model only computes the $Q$,",
